e2img01.jpg"}, {"time": "100", "text": "Because she is a computer programmer, Sherri is required to sit at a desk all day long. Since her posture is far from perfect, she was not surprised when she began to notice pain in her neck and shoulders. She assumed that she would probably have to learn to live with the problem, seeing as she worked long hours and that her job was stressful. She got a prescription for pain medication from her doctor and visited a good masseuse when the ache in her neck got very bad.\n<br/>\nOne day, seeing that Sherri was holding her neck stiffly, one of the other programmers asked her what was wrong. Sherri told him about her chronic neck and shoulder pain.\n\"Have you tried the Alexander technique?\" he asked.", "id": 1, "image": "engenn11le02ob03re2img01.jpg"}, {"time": "47600", "text": "Sherri had heard of the Alexander technique. It was said to be an effective cure, but she thought it was for those freaky, \"alternative\" types of people.\n<br/>\nThe Alexander technique is said to have been invented by F. M. Alexander (1869-1955). After almost losing his voice from laryngitis, Alexander, who was reported to have been an extraordinary Shakespearean actor, tried to find a way to strengthen his voice without conventional medical care. He carefully watched himself while speaking. He noticed that he was tightening his neck muscles so much that he could actually see the tension. When he learned to reduce neck tension and lengthen his spine, his vocal problem disappeared. From  his own experience and his work with others, Alexander is said to have created a teaching method that encourages the body to work more efficiently and holistically.", "id": 2, "image": "engenn11le02ob03re2img02.jpg"}, {"time": "107500", "text": "The Alexander technique is not easy, but it is said to be worth the effort. After using the technique for a year, Sherri has stopped complaining. \"I still put in crazy hours at work and spend a lot of time at the computer,\" says Sherri, \"but now when I notice that my neck is beginning to ache, I stop, think of what I learned, and adjust the way I'm sitting. I can feel the tension move up and out of me.\"", "id": 3, "image": "engenn11le02ob03re2img03.jpg"}], "coverimg": "engenn11le02ob03re2img01.jpg"}}], "template_override": 1, "type": "audio", "title": "", "_data": [], "id": 1}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}], "type": "lab_options"}], "id": 0}]}